<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Srimanta Sankardev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1449">1449</a>–<a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sankaradeva, Sankardev, Sankardeva, Srimanta Sankaradeva</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sankardev was an Assamese saint, scholar, playwright and social reformer. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekasarana-dharma">Ekasarana Dharma</a>, a Vaishnava devotional movement centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> that rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> barriers and idol worship, and established monasteries called sattras. His plays, songs and the dance tradition that grew from them, now known as Sattriya, shaped Assamese culture. In later life he found protection at the court of the Koch king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara-narayan">Nara Narayan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/srimanta-sankardev.jpg" alt="Srimanta Sankardev" width="320" height="506" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jrkalita · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sankaradeva.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
